      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fruitland was named to advertise its agricultural resources, and Euro‑American settlers were allowed in 1877.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://ehillerman.unm.edu/1587"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community is on the north side of the San Juan River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruitland%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Navajo term for Fruitland is Bááh Díílid, which means “burning bread”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://ehillerman.unm.edu/1587"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Burnham was made bishop of the LDS ward there in 1883, and the Young Stake was organized with headquarters at Burnham in 1912.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruitland%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area was also the site for the precursor of the Navajo Indian Irrigation Project.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://ehillerman.unm.edu/1587"
    },
    {
